class:: ShotNoise
summary:: Marked stochastic impulses with exponential memory
categories:: UGens>Generators>Stochastic>Processes
related:: Guides/Stochastica, Classes/RenewalTrig, Classes/Dust, Classes/Decay

description::

ShotNoise accumulates physical-like impulses. Each Poisson arrival adds a Gaussian amplitude mark to a decaying state. Sparse arrivals give isolated impacts; overlapping arrivals become crackle, rain, and dense agitation. Rate controls particle density, decay controls memory, and the amplitude distribution controls the shots. link::Classes/RenewalTrig:: exposes event timing, whereas ShotNoise also carries the summed response.

The model is code::x(t) = sum A_k * exp(-(t-t_k)/decay):: with independent Poisson arrivals and code::A_k ~ Normal(ampMean, ampStd^2)::. This is classical marked Poisson shot noise; the stationary identities follow from Campbell's theorem (Campbell, The study of discontinuous phenomena, 1909, link::https://archive.org/details/proceedingsofcam15190810camb/page/117/mode/1up::). For positive decay, mean is code::rate * ampMean * decay:: and variance is code::rate * (ampMean^2 + ampStd^2) * decay / 2::.

Integrated exponential hazards locate arrivals inside a calculation. The signal decays analytically to each arrival and through the remaining time. At most eight arrivals are processed per calculation. Unprocessed time is carried forward and uses the next calculation's parameters; overload delays model time and is approximate under modulation. Keep individual-shot rates far below the calculation rate. eventTrigger only indicates whether at least one arrival occurred.

Nonpositive decay sums instantaneous marks for the current calculation only. Rate zero permits an existing positive-decay tail to finish. Negative/non-finite rate becomes zero, non-finite decay becomes instantaneous, and non-finite amplitude inputs become zero. Non-finite or float-unrepresentable accumulated state clears the signal and schedule without reseeding. Ordinary output is unbounded. Reset starts empty; stationary statistics require several decay constants of warmup.

Like link::Classes/Dust:: into link::Classes/Decay:: it makes decaying random impacts, but it accounts for within-interval arrival times, Gaussian marks, and multiple accumulated shots.

